I couldn't find examples on how to fit gaussian processes with other (non-gaussian) likelihoods, so tried the obvious solution myself - replace all MvNormal with MvStudentT (with some fixed nu, for example I set 1).

First I subclassed GP class like this:

class GP(pm.gp.GP):
    def logp(self, Y, X=None):
        if X is None:
            X = self.X
        mu = self.M(X).squeeze()
        Sigma = self.K(X) + tt.eye(X.shape[0])*self.sigma**2

        return pm.MvStudentT.dist(mu=mu, Sigma=Sigma, nu=1).logp(Y)

then in sample_gp (gp/gp.py) replaced MvNormal.dist(m_post, S_post, shape=Z.shape[0]) with MvStudentT.dist(mu=m_post, Sigma=S_post, nu=1, shape=Z.shape[0]).

The model fits and samples successfully, but I'm totally not sure if this is the right solution, as the resulting process doesn't seem any more robust than plain pm.gp.GP. What's wrong in my solution?

Most of the time it takes to import pymc is spend in pymc3.theanof initializing something related to the theano rng: https://github.com/pymc-devs/pymc3/blob/master/pymc3/theanof.py#L374
On my machine it spends ~4 of a total of 5s there. Seems a bit excessive.
@ferrine That was introduced in #1694. Any idea why this would take so long?
